            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details open class="border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl bg-white shadow-sm">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer select-none px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-slate-900 hover:bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl">
            8b · Propensity-score calibration <span class="text-xs font-normal text-slate-400">· correcting a claims study with a richer validation sample</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-5 space-y-4">
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              A claims database records diagnoses, procedures, dispensings and demographics; it does not record lab values, vitals,
              imaging measurements, severity scores or lifestyle. A propensity score fitted there is therefore <em>error-prone</em>.
              If you also have a smaller dataset holding <strong>both</strong> the claims variables and the missing clinical ones, you can
              fit a <em>gold-standard</em> propensity score in it, learn how the two relate, and correct the large study's effect estimate.
              That is propensity-score calibration — regression calibration with the propensity score as the mismeasured covariate
              (Stürmer T, Schneeweiss S, Avorn J, Glynn RJ. <em>Am J Epidemiol</em> 2005;162(3):279-89).
              The fitted equation is the deliverable: a later claims-only study can cite it and apply it without repeating the validation work.
            </p>
            <label class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm border border-brand-200 bg-brand-50/50 rounded px-3 py-2 cursor-pointer">
              <input type="checkbox" name="pscEnable" value="yes" class="mt-0.5 rounded border-slate-400 text-brand-700 focus:ring-brand-500" />
              <span><strong>Include a propensity-score-calibration validation sub-study in this protocol.</strong>
                <span class="block text-[11px] text-slate-500">Adds Section 7b (variable split, calibration equation, assumptions, R code) to the Word and Markdown exports.</span></span>
            </label>

            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3">
              <label class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Main (error-prone) data source</span>
                <input type="text" name="pscClaimsDb" value="Taiwan NHIRD" placeholder="e.g. Taiwan NHIRD"
                       class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-3 py-2 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500" />
                <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-500">The large claims database the main analysis runs in.</span>
              </label>
              <label class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Validation dataset</span>
                <input type="text" name="pscValName" placeholder="e.g. Chang Gung Research Database; hospital EMR cohort"
                       class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-3 py-2 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500" />
                <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-500">Holds the claims variables <em>and</em> the clinical ones.</span>
              </label>
              <label class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Validation sample size</span>
                <input type="number" name="pscValN" min="0" step="1" placeholder="e.g. 654"
                       class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-3 py-2 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500" />
              </label>
              <label class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Validation sample type</span>
                <select name="pscValType" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-3 py-2 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500">
                  <option value="internal" selected>Internal (a subset of the main study cohort)</option>
                  <option value="external">External (a separate but comparable population)</option>
                </select>
              </label>
              <label class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">PS scale (must match the outcome model)</span>
                <select name="pscScale" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-3 py-2 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500">
                  <option value="ps" selected>Probability scale, 0-1 — as in the original method</option>
                  <option value="logit">logit(PS), linear predictor — a deviation; justify it</option>
                </select>
                <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-500">Stürmer's method uses the propensity score as a probability and a <em>linear</em> calibration model; keep the default unless you have a reason. Either way the correction is scale-dependent, so both models must use the same scale.</span>
              </label>
            </div>

            <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-3 bg-slate-50/60 space-y-3">
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 flex-wrap">
                <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Which covariates does the claims database actually have?</p>
                <div class="flex gap-1.5 flex-wrap">
                  <button type="button" data-psc-ai class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest font-bold text-brand-700 bg-brand-50 hover:bg-brand-100 border border-brand-200 px-2 py-1 rounded transition">🤖 Let AI classify</button>
                  <button type="button" data-psc-bulk="reset" class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest font-bold text-slate-600 border border-slate-300 hover:bg-white px-2 py-1 rounded transition">Reset to rules</button>
                  <button type="button" data-psc-bulk="claims" class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest font-bold text-slate-600 border border-slate-300 hover:bg-white px-2 py-1 rounded transition">All claims</button>
                  <button type="button" data-psc-bulk="clinical" class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest font-bold text-slate-600 border border-slate-300 hover:bg-white px-2 py-1 rounded transition">All clinical</button>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
                Every covariate ticked in Section 8, plus anything you add below. Click a chip to move a variable between the two propensity scores.
                <strong>Claims</strong> variables go into both scores; <strong>clinical-only</strong> variables go into the gold-standard score alone; <strong>exclude</strong> drops
                identifiers, outcomes and post-baseline measurements from both.
              </p>
              <p class="text-[11px] text-amber-800 bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded px-2 py-1.5 leading-relaxed">
                <strong>The keyword rule is only a starting position, and it is wrong often enough to matter.</strong> It reads variable names, so it cannot tell a
                measurement from a billing code, and it misses instruments it has never seen. Run <em>Let AI classify</em> and check every row it marks <em>AI changed</em> —
                a clinical variable left in the claims list quietly collapses the correction to zero, which looks exactly like "no unmeasured confounding".
              </p>
              <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-600 bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded px-2 py-1.5 leading-relaxed">
                <strong>What the AI button sends.</strong> Variable names from Section 8 and from the two boxes below, your codebook notes, the name of the claims
                database, and the four research-question fields (population, exposure, comparator, outcome) — all of it verbatim. Nothing else leaves the browser:
                no patient data, no rows, no values. Keep patient-identifiable text out of every one of those fields.
              </p>
              <div data-psc-vars class="grid gap-1"></div>
              <p data-psc-ai-msg class="text-[11px]"></p>
              <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3">
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                  <span class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-500 font-semibold">Variables only the validation dataset has (one per line)</span>
                  <textarea name="pscVarsExtra" rows="4" placeholder="HbA1c (first &amp; last)&#10;Serum creatinine / eGFR&#10;BMI, height, weight&#10;Corrected visual acuity&#10;Duration of diabetes"
                            class="w-full border border-slate-300 rounded px-3 py-2 text-xs focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500"></textarea>
                </label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                  <span class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-500 font-semibold">Codebook notes for the AI (<code>Variable | definition</code>)</span>
                  <textarea name="pscNotes" rows="4" placeholder="HbA1c_first | most recent value before the first injection&#10;DM duration | index date minus earliest diabetes diagnosis (years)"
                            class="w-full border border-slate-300 rounded px-3 py-2 text-xs focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500"></textarea>
                  <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-500">Definitions only — see the note above on what is sent.</span>
                </label>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border border-rose-200 bg-rose-50/50 rounded-xl p-3 space-y-2">
              <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-rose-800 font-semibold">Step 0 · The surrogacy test — do this before you trust any correction</p>
              <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
                Calibration only works if the claims-only score carries no information about the outcome beyond the gold-standard score.
                Wan (<em>Am J Epidemiol</em> 2024) shows this holds only when the covariates' effects on the outcome are <strong>proportional</strong> to their effects
                on treatment; otherwise the correction is biased and can be <em>worse than doing nothing</em>. If your validation dataset has outcomes, measure it.
              </p>
              <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
                The measure to use is <strong>S</strong>, from Lunt et al. (<em>Am J Epidemiol</em> 2012, Table 2 footnote g): of the outcome variation explained by both
                propensity scores together, the percentage attributable to the gold-standard score alone. 100% means perfect surrogacy. It is a ratio of two
                likelihood-ratio statistics and the R code below computes it. <strong>Prefer it to a p-value</strong> — in a validation sample of any size the p-value
                rejects on departures far too small to matter, and our own simulation found it rejecting at S = 92.5% where calibration still removed most of the bias.
              </p>
              <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-2">
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">S, surrogacy % (0-100)</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" min="0" max="100" name="pscSurroS" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">Coefficient on PS_ep</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" name="pscSurroCoef" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">Its p-value (secondary)</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" min="0" max="1" name="pscSurroP" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">Validation outcomes available?</span>
                  <select name="pscSurroTestable" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none">
                    <option value="yes" selected>Yes — surrogacy is measurable</option>
                    <option value="no">No — surrogacy is unmeasurable here</option>
                  </select></label>
              </div>
              <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                Anchors, from Lunt's published Table 2: scenarios with S of 88-99% gave 75-114% bias reduction, while S of 6-14% gave bias reduction of
                −132% to −189% — calibration made things substantially worse. In our own simulation PSC helped at S ≥ 92% (5/5 replicates) and hurt at S ≈ 82%
                (0/5). The bands this tool uses are a reading of those two sources, not a published cut-off.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-3 space-y-3">
              <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Fitted coefficients (fill in once the validation analysis has run)</p>
              <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
                From the calibration model <code>PS_gs ~ PS_ep + treatment</code> in the validation dataset, and from the claims outcome model
                <code>outcome ~ treatment + PS_ep</code>. Leave blank at the protocol stage — the section still exports with the equation in symbolic form.
              </p>
              <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-2">
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">α₀ intercept</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" name="pscA0" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">α₁ on PS_ep</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" name="pscA1" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">SE(α₁)</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" name="pscA1se" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">α₂ on treatment</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" name="pscA2" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">SE(α₂)</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" name="pscA2se" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">Validation R²</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" name="pscR2" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">β̂ treatment (claims, naive)</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" name="pscBt" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">SE(β̂ treatment)</span>
                  <input type="number" step="any" name="pscBtse" class="border border-slate-300 rounded px-2 py-1.5 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none" /></label>
                <label class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-slate-700">β̂ PS_ep (claims, naive)</span>
